1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,878
Separable equations are the first real solving method in this

2
00:00:03,878 --> 00:00:07,208
course, and honestly, they are the friendliest one.

3
00:00:08,228 --> 00:00:10,865
Here is the whole idea in one sentence.

4
00:00:10,865 --> 00:00:14,019
If you can push every y to one side, and every x to

5
00:00:14,019 --> 00:00:17,287
the other side, you just integrate both sides.

6
00:00:17,707 --> 00:00:19,998
That is it. That is the method.

7
00:00:19,998 --> 00:00:24,123
But the details are exactly where marks get lost, so let us go slowly.

8
00:00:27,823 --> 00:00:29,779
So how do you recognize one?

9
00:00:29,779 --> 00:00:33,132
Look at the right-hand side and ask: can I write it as a

10
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:36,373
function of x only, times a function of y only?

11
00:00:36,793 --> 00:00:40,282
Your lecture has a great example: dy/dx equals x

12
00:00:40,282 --> 00:00:45,046
squared, y to the fourth, e to the five x minus three y.

13
00:00:45,466 --> 00:00:48,801
It looks tangled, but that exponential splits.

14
00:00:48,801 --> 00:00:54,359
e to the five x minus three y, is e to the five x, times e to the minus three y.

15
00:00:54,779 --> 00:00:58,736
Now group them. The x stuff is x squared e to the five x.

16
00:00:58,736 --> 00:01:02,414
The y stuff is y to the fourth, e to the minus three y.

17
00:01:02,414 --> 00:01:05,145
That is a product, so it is separable.

18
00:01:05,566 --> 00:01:09,847
Compare that with dy/dx equals y, plus cosine x.

19
00:01:10,266 --> 00:01:12,334
That plus sign is the problem.

20
00:01:12,334 --> 00:01:17,642
A sum cannot be split into an x piece times a y piece, so this one is not separable.

21
00:01:17,642 --> 00:01:20,380
It is linear, which is the next video.

22
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:27,391
Here is the method, exactly the way your lecture slides write it.

23
00:01:27,811 --> 00:01:32,133
Start with dy/dx equals g of x, times h of y.

24
00:01:32,552 --> 00:01:34,661
Divide both sides by h of y.

25
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:38,698
Your professor gives one over h of y a new name: p of y.

26
00:01:39,118 --> 00:01:44,237
Then treat dy/dx like a fraction, and move the dx across.

27
00:01:44,237 --> 00:01:47,173
p of y dy, equals g of x dx.

28
00:01:47,593 --> 00:01:50,371
Now each side only knows about one variable.

29
00:01:50,371 --> 00:01:54,075
So integrate each side, with respect to its own variable.

30
00:01:54,495 --> 00:01:57,014
And here is the detail people forget.

31
00:01:57,014 --> 00:01:59,819
You only need one constant of integration.

32
00:01:59,819 --> 00:02:02,051
Put a single plus C on one side.

33
00:02:02,471 --> 00:02:08,086
One more thing. Before you divide by h of y, ask: where is h of y equal to zero?

34
00:02:08,086 --> 00:02:12,601
Those values give constant solutions, and dividing can throw them away.

35
00:02:12,601 --> 00:02:16,069
Keep that in your pocket. It matters in a minute.

36
00:02:19,369 --> 00:02:22,393
Let us do Example 3 from Lecture 3.

37
00:02:22,393 --> 00:02:25,345
dy/dx equals y squared minus four.

38
00:02:25,765 --> 00:02:30,100
Here g of x is just one, and h of y is y squared minus four.

39
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:33,474
Step zero. Where is h equal to zero?

40
00:02:33,474 --> 00:02:38,790
y squared minus four equals zero gives y equals two, and y equals negative two.

41
00:02:38,790 --> 00:02:42,571
Those are constant solutions. Write them down now.

42
00:02:42,991 --> 00:02:47,449
Now separate. dy over y squared minus four, equals dx.

43
00:02:47,869 --> 00:02:51,576
To integrate the left side, use partial fractions.

44
00:02:51,576 --> 00:02:55,223
One over y squared minus four, is one quarter times,

45
00:02:55,223 --> 00:02:58,809
one over y minus two, minus one over y plus two.

46
00:02:59,229 --> 00:03:04,555
Integrate both sides. One quarter, natural log of the absolute value of y

47
00:03:04,555 --> 00:03:08,232
minus two over y plus two, equals x plus c one.

48
00:03:08,652 --> 00:03:11,459
Multiply by four, and exponentiate.

49
00:03:11,459 --> 00:03:14,144
The constant comes along as a factor.

50
00:03:14,144 --> 00:03:18,415
y minus two over y plus two, equals c, e to the four x.

51
00:03:18,835 --> 00:03:23,017
Solve for y, and you get: y equals two times, one plus c

52
00:03:23,017 --> 00:03:26,768
e to the four x, over one minus c e to the four x.

53
00:03:27,189 --> 00:03:29,681
Now check those constant solutions.

54
00:03:29,681 --> 00:03:32,945
Put c equals zero, and you get y equals two.

55
00:03:32,945 --> 00:03:36,030
So y equals two is already inside the family.

56
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:40,658
But y equals negative two? There is no value of c that gives it.

57
00:03:40,658 --> 00:03:45,675
It is a singular solution, and you must list it separately, or you lose marks.

58
00:03:46,095 --> 00:03:48,300
Here is what that looks like.

59
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:53,139
The lines y equals two and y equals negative two are equilibrium solutions,

60
00:03:53,139 --> 00:03:57,855
and every other solution curve lives between them, or beyond them.

61
00:04:02,355 --> 00:04:04,637
Two traps show up on almost every exam.

62
00:04:05,057 --> 00:04:09,226
Trap one. Forgetting the constant solutions you divided away.

63
00:04:09,226 --> 00:04:12,784
Always check where h of y is zero, before you divide.

64
00:04:13,204 --> 00:04:16,468
Trap two. The constant in the exponent.

65
00:04:16,468 --> 00:04:22,318
If natural log of y equals G of x plus C, then y is not, e to the G, plus C.

66
00:04:22,738 --> 00:04:25,252
Exponentiate the whole right side.

67
00:04:25,252 --> 00:04:30,219
y equals e to the G plus C, which is e to the C, times e to the G.

68
00:04:30,219 --> 00:04:32,304
So the constant multiplies.

69
00:04:36,104 --> 00:04:38,934
So here is the recipe to take into the midterm.

70
00:04:39,354 --> 00:04:43,729
One. Check that the right side is a product, g of x times h of y.

71
00:04:44,149 --> 00:04:46,334
Two. Find where h of y is zero.

72
00:04:46,334 --> 00:04:48,324
Those are your constant solutions.

73
00:04:48,744 --> 00:04:52,465
Three. Separate, integrate both sides, and add one constant.

74
00:04:52,885 --> 00:04:56,744
Four. Solve for y, apply the initial condition, and make

75
00:04:56,744 --> 00:04:59,625
sure every constant solution is accounted for.

76
00:05:00,045 --> 00:05:01,803
That is separable equations.

77
00:05:01,803 --> 00:05:05,872
Next up, first-order linear equations, and the integrating factor.
